findings.body:
**Issue:** `execFile` (no shell, argv array) was replaced with `exec` and a shell template that interpolates `src` and `filter`. Any caller-controlled value becomes shell syntax.

**Failure mode:** `filter` is unquoted, so `filter = "Lanczos; id > /tmp/pwned"` runs `id` after `convert`. `src` is only double-quoted, so a path like `"; id; echo "` also escapes. On a service that resizes user uploads or accepts filter names from a query string, this is remote code execution on the worker.

**Fix:** keep a non-shell spawn and pass arguments as a list. Validate `filter` against an allowlist of ImageMagick filter names if “power user” composition is required:

```ts
import { execFile } from "child_process";

const ALLOWED_FILTERS = new Set(["Lanczos", "Mitchell", "Catrom", "Cubic", "Triangle", "Point"]);

export function resize(src: string, filter: string, w: number): Promise<string> {
  if (!ALLOWED_FILTERS.has(filter)) {
    return Promise.reject(new Error(`unsupported filter: ${filter}`));
  }
  return new Promise((res, rej) => {
    execFile("convert", [src, "-filter", filter, "-resize", `${w}x`, "/tmp/out.png"], (e) =>
      e ? rej(e) : res("/tmp/out.png")
    );
  });
}
```

A shell is not required for `-filter`; argv form already supports it safely.
